      "textEn": "146\n\nIs separate from God, He is very dear to Lord Vishnu.\"\n\nOf the mind, without the desire to know,\nI have directly experienced the suffering. Without the illusion of the Supreme Being,\nI will not be freed from the endless suffering. 27. Meaning of the word\n\nManah - mind; Vachah - speech; Drigah - sight; Karanah - of the senses; Ihitasya - of all actions (to maintain body, society, friendship, etc.); Sakhat-kritam - directly given; Me - to me; Paribarhanam - worship; Hi - because; Vina - without; Puman - any person; Yena - by which; Maha-vimohat - from great delusion; Kritant-pashat - from the noose of Yama (god of death); Na - not; Vimoktum - to be freed; Isets - is capable. Mind, sight, speech, and all sense organs, and organs of action, the real function of the senses is to remain engaged in my service. Until the senses of the soul are not served in this way, it is difficult for the soul to\nget out of worldly bonds like the noose of Yamraj.\nMeaning: It is said in Narada-Pancharatra—\n\nThat which is liberated from all obstacles, is pure by its very nature. Hrishikesha, serving Hrishikesha is called devotion. That is the essence of devotion. Lord Rishabhdev was constantly emphasizing devotion, and now, in the end, this\nsays that the senses should be engaged in the service of God. We acquire knowledge through the five senses,\nand we perform actions through these same senses. These ten senses and the mind should be fully engaged in the service of God.\nShould be engaged. Without doing this, one cannot get rid of the clutches of Maya.\nSreesukha said,\n\nThus, instructing the self-aware, he, being greatly kind, instructed the great sages for the sake of instructing the world, the supreme friend, the Lord of the earth.\nThe great sages, who are endowed with patience, forbearance, and other qualities, are taught the path of devotion, knowledge, and detachment.\nThe best of the self-realized, the best of the wise, the supreme devotee of t