      "textEn": "14\n\nHe was in the past, not in the present and future. Similarly, wise people are always striving for liberation and\nwant to be one with the Supreme Soul. Yogis are behind many siddhis, such as Anima, Lagima,\nPrapti, etc. But the devotee has no interest in these things, because he is completely dependent on the grace of Shri Krishna. Krishna is Yogeshwar, the Lord of all Yogas (Siddhis). They are self-content.\nThat is, they are self-satisfied. This verse describes the yogic practices. With these, a human can fly in space without any equipment\nand can travel with the speed of the mind. This means that when\na yogi wishes to go somewhere inside or outside this universe, he immediately reaches there.\nHe goes. It is difficult to determine the speed of the mind, because in a moment the mind goes millions of miles. Sometimes, accomplished yogis perform actions according to their wishes by entering other bodies.\nUpon growing old,\nSometimes a proven yogi can attain a youthful and strong body. By abandoning his old body,\nHe can enter the young body and act according to his will. Being a part of Lord Vasudev,\nRishabhdev had all these yogic powers, but he was satisfied only by the devotion to Shri Krishna.\nWhose manifestation was through his laughing, crying, or trembling. Thus, under the fifth canto of Srimad Bhagavat, the Bhakti Vedanta meaning of the fifth chapter titled \"Bhagwan Rishabhdev's Teachings to his sons\" is completed. Chapter Six:\n\nThe activities of Lord Rishabhdev\n\nIn this chapter, there is a description of Lord Rishabhdev's death. When his body was burning in the fire, even then he was not attached to his body. When the seed of karma is burned by the fire of knowledge,\nthen divine qualities and yogic powers manifest spontaneously, but even then, these yogic powers have no effect on devotion.\nA common Yogi becomes fascinated by the powers of Yoga, and his progress stops.\nTherefore, a Siddha Yogi does not consider them good. 