      "textEn": "161\n\nOf that, this body of the liberated one, the divine Rishabha, by the power of Yoga, is born in this world, without pride.\nHe, ascending, approaching Konkavekutakan, from the southern Karnataka region, with joy, the grove of Kutakachalo, the beautiful one.\nLike a pot of inertness, the liberated one, with his head held high, is seen moving. 7>. Meaning of the word\n\nTasya—Of that (God Rishabhadeva)—Ha va—As if; Evam—In this way; Mukta-lignasya—Of one whose gross and subtle bodies\nhave no identity with the body; Bhagavatah—Of God; Rishabhasya—Of Rishabhadeva; Yoga-maya-vasanaya—For the sake of God's plays;\nFrom the attainment of Yoga-maya; Deha—Body; Imam—This; Jagatim—Earth; Abhiman-abhasena—This body is made of material elements,\nFrom this kind of illusion; Sankramamanah—Wandering; Konk-vek-kutakan—Konk, Venk and Kutak; South—South\nIn India; Karnataka—In the state of Karnataka; Desh—All countries; Yadrichaya—By own will; Upaghatah—Reached; Kutakachal-\nUpanave—In the forest near Kutakachal; Asya—Inside the mouth; Krut-Ashmakavalah—Stone filled in the mouth; Unmadah Iva—Like madness\nLike a madman; mukt-murdhayu - with free hair; anasvī - naked; eva - like; vichāra - began to wander..\n\nIn reality, Lord Rishabhdev did not have a physical body, but through Yogmaya, he was considering his body to be physical. Therefore, by behaving like a common man, they renounced such\nmentalities. He set out on a world tour. While wandering, they reached the Karnataka region of South India,\nwhere Konk, Venk, and Kutak fell on the way. In this direction, to roam,\nThey had no plan, but near Kuttakal, they entered a forest. They filled their mouths with pieces of stone and started wandering in the forest like a madman, naked and with hair scattered.\nThen, with the wind, the flute, the rain, the fire, the forest, the garland, and the fire, he gives.8>. Meaning of the word\n\nAth—thereafter; Samir-veg—with the gust of wind; Vidhut—thrilled; Venu—of bamboos; Vikarshan—from the rubbing; Jaat—produced;\nHui; Ugra—strong; Daava-analah—fire of Daava; Tat—that; Vanam—the forest near Kutakachal; Aleleehanah—all around\neating from; Sah—with; Tena—that body; Dadah—burnt to ashes. While they were wandering, a strong fire of passion broke out. This fire was produced by the friction of the bamboos that were scattered by the wind. In this fire, the entire forest of Kutakachal and the body of Rishabhdev\nwere reduced to ashes. Meaning: Such a fire can burn the bodies of animals, but Lord Rishabhdev did not burn,\nthough externally it seemed that he was burned. Lord Rishabhdev is the supreme soul of all beings in the world,\nand their soul can never burn in fire. As it is said in the Bhagavad Gita"
